## Result up front

The proposed sharp hard-exclusion shortcut does not work on the first exact
partial-overlap witness.

Block24 supplies a conditional append instrument for one complete current
Record followed by one exact radial Blank block. A tempting global extension
is to form sharp eligibility projectors for every possible tip, jointly
diagonalize them, activate the isolated tips, and send every colliding sector
to identity. That extension requires the overlapping eligibility projectors to
commute.

The literal physical factors give a clean counterexample. Choose

```text
front A   = (-1, 0, 0)       anchor A = (0, 0, 0)
front B   = ( 1, 0, 0)       anchor B = (-19, -1, 0)
target A  = (-9, 0, 0)       target B = (-10, -1, 0)
target shift = (-1, -1, 0).
```

The two current carriers are disjoint, and neither current carrier intersects
the other tip's target. Only the two target carriers overlap, on exactly two
constrained qubits. Their literal one-qubit state fidelities are

\[
q_1=q_2=\frac12,
\qquad q=q_1q_2=\frac14.                              \tag{1}
\]

For rank-one projectors `P` and `Q`, with
`q=Tr(PQ)=|<p|q>|^2`,

\[
[P,Q]=0 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q\in\{0,1\},
\qquad
\operatorname{Tr}([P,Q]^\dagger[P,Q])=2q(1-q).       \tag{2}
\]

Equations (1)--(2) give

\[
\operatorname{Tr}([P,Q]^\dagger[P,Q])=\frac38>0.     \tag{3}
\]

The runner also constructs the two shared-qubit matrices directly and obtains
the same `3/8`, independently of the scalar formula. This shared-carrier
calculation also lifts to the complete two-tip Hilbert space. Writing the two
full eligibility projectors as

\[
 \Pi_A=A\otimes P_s\otimes I_B,
 \qquad
 \Pi_B=I_A\otimes Q_s\otimes B,                         \tag{4}
\]

where `A` and `B` are the nonzero rank-one projectors on each tip's unique
factors, gives

\[
 [\Pi_A,\Pi_B]=A\otimes[P_s,Q_s]\otimes B.              \tag{5}
\]

Because `A` and `B` are rank-one projectors,
`Tr(A^2)Tr(B^2)=1`, so its squared Hilbert--Schmidt norm is again `3/8`.
Thus the full eligibility projectors do not commute, and this pair has no one
joint sharp eligibility-sector decomposition.

